-----BEGIN PREVIOUS OUTPUT-----
Confirmed. Line 830 uses `all_finite` in a condition (`if self.metric != "precomputed" and not all_finite`), but `all_finite` is only assigned in the `if self.metric != "precomputed"` branch (lines 699-712). If `self.metric != "precomputed"` is True, `all_finite` is defined. So the short-circuit `not "precomputed"` protects it. OK.

But `finite_index`, `internal_to_raw`, `infinite_index`, `missing_index` are only defined when `not all_finite`. Line 830-838 uses them under the correct guard. Fine.

Now, `reduced_X` at line 721 is `X.sum(axis=1)` which for a sparse LiL/CSR gives a matrix; `np.isnan(reduced_X).nonzero()[0]` — for a sparse matrix, `.sum(axis=1)` returns a matrix and `np.isnan` works but `.nonzero()` behavior differs. Not going to dig; test coverage likely handles this.

Let me check `_get_finite_row_indices` — it uses `matrix.tolil().data` which iterates only the *stored* values, missing any structurally-zero entries. But if the sparse matrix is a distance matrix, most zeros are valid distances (self-distance) or explicit zero distances. The function is called only in the non-precomputed path though, so distance matrix concerns aren't at play. Actually, `_get_finite_row_indices` is used for feature matrices in the non-precomputed path. In a sparse feature matrix, structural zeros mean "value is 0" (finite), so ignoring them is correct. OK.

